Prayer is one of the most important ways Christians grow in their relationship with God. While many believers are familiar with spontaneous or personal prayer, the church has also practiced **structured prayer** for centuries. Structured prayer includes written prayers, liturgical responses, and historic patterns of prayer that guide believers in worship.

At **Resurrection Anglican Church in Rock Hill, SC**, structured prayer is an important part of our worship life. Far from being mechanical or restrictive, these prayers often deepen a believer’s spiritual life and help cultivate a consistent walk with Christ.

Here are **ten ways structured prayer can help you grow spiritually**.

## 1. It Teaches You How to Pray

Many Christians struggle with knowing what to say when they pray. Structured prayers give believers language that has been shaped by Scripture and centuries of Christian devotion.

Just as Jesus gave His disciples the **Lord’s Prayer** as a model, structured prayers help teach us how to approach God with reverence, confession, thanksgiving, and petition.

## 2. It Grounds Your Prayers in Scripture

Many historic prayers are filled with biblical language and themes. As you pray them regularly, you begin to internalize Scripture and allow it to shape your thinking and spiritual life.

Over time, structured prayer helps believers develop a **Bible-shaped prayer life**.

## 3. It Keeps Prayer From Becoming Self-Centered

Left to ourselves, our prayers can sometimes become focused primarily on our own needs and concerns.

Structured prayers remind us to pray for **the church, the world, the suffering, and God’s kingdom**, expanding our vision beyond our own circumstances.

## 4. It Builds Spiritual Discipline

Spiritual growth often requires consistency. Structured prayer creates a rhythm that helps believers pray regularly rather than only when they feel like it.

Just as regular exercise strengthens the body, consistent prayer strengthens the soul.

## 5. It Connects You to the Historic Church

When you pray historic prayers used by Christians for generations, you are joining a long line of believers who have prayed the same words.

At **Resurrection Anglican Church in Rock Hill, SC**, our prayers often reflect the rich heritage of the historic Christian faith, connecting us to the global church across centuries.

## 6. It Shapes Your Heart Over Time

The words we repeat regularly begin to shape our hearts and attitudes. When believers regularly pray prayers of confession, humility, and gratitude, those virtues begin to grow in their lives.

Structured prayer gently forms the soul over time.

## 7. It Helps During Spiritually Dry Seasons

Every believer goes through times when prayer feels difficult. During these seasons, structured prayers provide a guide when we struggle to find words of our own.

Many Christians have found that written prayers help sustain their faith during difficult spiritual seasons.

## 8. It Encourages Corporate Worship

Structured prayer allows an entire congregation to pray together in unity. Instead of one person praying while others listen, everyone participates.

This shared prayer strengthens the sense of community within the church.

## 9. It Keeps God at the Center

Structured prayers often focus on **God’s character, His holiness, and His saving work in Christ**. This helps keep our attention on who God is rather than only on what we want from Him.

True prayer begins with worship.

## 10. It Leads You Through the Story of the Gospel

Many structured prayers follow the natural movement of the Christian life:

- Adoration of God
- Confession of sin
- Assurance of forgiveness
- Thanksgiving
- Intercession for others

This rhythm reflects the **story of the Gospel itself**, reminding believers again and again of God’s grace in Jesus Christ.

## Resources for Practicing Structured Prayer

If you would like to begin incorporating structured prayer into your daily life, there are several helpful resources available. One of the best ways to start is by praying the **Daily Office**, a historic pattern of morning and evening prayer used by Christians for centuries.

You can easily follow the Daily Office using the **Daily Office 2019**. This free app provides guided morning and evening prayers based on the **2019 Book of Common Prayer**. It includes Scripture readings, Psalms, and historic prayers that help believers develop a consistent rhythm of prayer.

You can explore it here:\
\
[**https://www.dailyoffice2019.com/**](https://www.dailyoffice2019.com/)

If you would like the full prayer book used in Anglican churches today, you can download the **Book of Common Prayer 2019** as a free PDF here:\
\
[**https://bcp2019.anglicanchurch.net/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/BCP2019.pdf**](https://bcp2019.anglicanchurch.net/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/BCP2019.pdf)

These resources can help guide your prayer life and connect you to the rich spiritual tradition of the historic church. Many members of **Resurrection Anglican Church in Rock Hill, SC** use these prayers daily as part of their spiritual walk with Christ.

# Growing Spiritually Through Prayer

Structured prayer is not meant to replace personal prayer, but to **strengthen and guide it**. For centuries, Christians have discovered that these historic patterns of prayer help cultivate deeper faith, humility, and devotion to Christ.

At **Resurrection Anglican Church in Rock Hill, South Carolina**, structured prayer is part of a larger rhythm of worship centered on Scripture, communion, and the historic practices of the Christian faith.

If you are seeking a deeper prayer life, structured prayer may become one of the most powerful tools for spiritual growth.

We invite you to join us and experience worship rooted in Scripture, tradition, and the transforming grace of God.
